\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Example}
\title{Why the product spin basis passes both trace tests}
\date{2026-11-26}

\p{On the spin space in \ref{fdip-00LB}, the #{j}th generator
flips bit #{j} of a basis vector and multiplies it by signs
from earlier bits. A nonempty increasing word therefore flips
each bit of its subset once, sending every basis vector to a
different one. Its trace vanishes. The empty word has real
trace #{\dim S}. Both signs pass \ref{fdip-017K}.
The two explicit antiunitaries of \ref{fdip-013T} have
square #{\bbI_S} on this product space.}
